WEBVTT

00:00:00.586 --> 00:00:01.436
<v><b>Narrator:</b></v> 1

00:00:03.507 --> 00:00:08.382
<v><b>T:</b></v> OK, so now repeat after me – <i>du pain</i>

00:00:09.136 --> 00:00:10.436
<v><b>SS:</b></v> <i>du pain</i>

00:00:11.158 --> 00:00:13.017
<v><b>T:</b></v> <i>de la salade</i>

00:00:13.382 --> 00:00:15.382
<v><b>SS:</b></v> <i>de la salade</i>

00:00:15.851 --> 00:00:17.177
<v><b>T:</b></v> <i>des saucisses</i>

00:00:17.693 --> 00:00:19.693
<v><b>SS:</b></v> <i>des saucisses</i>

00:00:22.930 --> 00:00:23.692
<v><b>Narrator:</b></v> 2

00:00:25.858 --> 00:00:32.134
<v><b>T:</b></v> So last week we looked at percentages, and today we’re going to look at fractions.

00:00:32.690 --> 00:00:35.934
Can someone give me an example of a fraction?

00:00:36.323 --> 00:00:38.323
<v><b>S:</b></v> Er, three quarters?

00:00:38.497 --> 00:00:43.132
<v><b>T:</b></v> Yes, exactly. And what would that be as a percentage?

00:00:43.322 --> 00:00:45.184
<v><b>S:</b></v> 75%.

00:00:48.589 --> 00:00:49.428
<v><b>Narrator:</b></v> 3

00:00:51.404 --> 00:00:55.749
<v><b>T:</b></v> Can anybody tell me the dates of the First World War?

00:00:55.773 --> 00:00:58.507
<v><b>S:</b></v> Oh, me sir, me sir!

00:00:59.379 --> 00:01:00.403
<v><b>T:</b></v> Angela?

00:01:00.633 --> 00:01:04.990
<v><b>S:</b></v> 1939 to 1945, sir.

00:01:05.593 --> 00:01:08.751
<v><b>T:</b></v> No, that’s the Second World War.

00:01:10.671 --> 00:01:12.302
Try again.

00:01:15.889 --> 00:01:16.761
<v><b>Narrator:</b></v> 4

00:01:18.895 --> 00:01:23.788
<v><b>T:</b></v> So we’re going to start by defining a few words and phrases.

00:01:24.058 --> 00:01:29.621
I’m going to dictate the terms and I want you to write a short definition.

00:01:29.970 --> 00:01:34.668
So, for example, if I said the word cookies, what would you write?

00:01:35.426 --> 00:01:36.442
<v><b>S1:</b></v> <i>Biscuits?</i>

00:01:36.831 --> 00:01:39.467
<v><b>T:</b></v> Very funny, Carl. Anna?

00:01:39.959 --> 00:01:45.577
<v><b>S2:</b></v> Er, pieces of data that, er, websites put onto your computer?

00:01:45.926 --> 00:01:49.941
<v><b>T:</b></v> That’s right. And what do internet cookies do?

00:01:50.448 --> 00:01:56.527
<v><b>S2:</b></v> They record details of the websites you’ve visited and, er, how long you’ve spent on them.

00:01:58.788 --> 00:01:59.711
<v><b>Narrator:</b></v> 5

00:02:01.901 --> 00:02:06.001
<v><b>T:</b></v> OK, Act 3, scene 1. OK, Jack you can be Hamlet.

00:02:06.318 --> 00:02:09.175
This is a very important speech. Are you ready?

00:02:09.571 --> 00:02:20.529
<v><b>S:</b></v> Yes. <i>To be or not to be, that is the question. Whether…</i>

00:02:22.743 --> 00:02:23.718
<v><b>Narrator:</b></v> 6

00:02:25.765 --> 00:02:32.426
<v><b>T:</b></v> So I have a small piece of sodium here, about one cubic centimetre,

00:02:32.450 --> 00:02:35.521
and we’re going to see what happens when we add it to water.

00:02:35.545 --> 00:02:43.814
So if you could all stand back a bit…further back…OK…here we go…

00:02:50.933 --> 00:02:51.882
<v><b>Narrator:</b></v> 7

00:02:54.207 --> 00:03:00.892
<v><b>T:</b></v> Today, we’re going to look at two self-portraits from different centuries. Take a look at these.

00:03:01.392 --> 00:03:03.392
<v><b>S:</b></v> The one on the right looks like you, sir!

00:03:04.605 --> 00:03:10.105
<v><b>T:</b></v> Thank you, Jasper. Now I want you to start by thinking about two questions.

00:03:10.700 --> 00:03:13.167
When do you think the self-portraits were painted?

00:03:13.587 --> 00:03:17.349
And how old do you think the artists were when they painted them?

00:03:20.936 --> 00:03:21.761
<v><b>Narrator:</b></v> 8

00:03:23.769 --> 00:03:29.384
<v><b>T:</b></v> OK, we’re going to warm up first, I want you to run round the track three times.

00:03:29.542 --> 00:03:31.581
<v><b>SS:</b></v> Can’t we play football?

00:03:31.835 --> 00:03:37.017
<v><b>T:</b></v> No, come on, three times round the track. Ready, steady, go.

00:03:40.723 --> 00:03:41.621
<v><b>Narrator:</b></v> 9

00:03:44.240 --> 00:03:50.856
<v><b>T:</b></v> There are no active volcanoes in the UK, but there are quite a lot in the rest of Europe.

00:03:51.141 --> 00:03:55.783
Can anyone tell me a European country that has active volcanoes?

00:03:56.219 --> 00:03:58.598
<v><b>S1:</b></v> Erm, France?

00:03:59.233 --> 00:04:01.233
<v><b>T:</b></v> Not France, no…

00:04:01.669 --> 00:04:05.573
<v><b>S2:</b></v> Italy? Italy has Mount Etna.

00:04:05.986 --> 00:04:15.036
<v><b>T:</b></v> Yes, Italy. In fact, Etna is the highest active volcano in Europe, at over 3,300 metres.